"april99 (https://www.vos.noaa.gov/MWL/apr1999.pdf)\nciteturn22view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 108; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.vos.noaa.gov/MWL/apr1999.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 6538\nL0@P0: Vol. 43, No. 1 April 1999\nL1@P0: Mariners Weather Log\nL2@P0: Hurricane Mitch, one of the deadliest Atlantic hurricanes in history,\nL3@P0: as sea level pressure dropped to 905 Hp 35 nm southeast of Swan Island.\nL4@P0: This was the lowest sea level pressure ever observed in an October\nL5@P0: hurricane in the Atlantic basin and the fourth lowest pressure ever observed\nL6@P0-1: in an Atlantic hurricane (tied with Camille in 1969). See page 4.2 Mariners Weather Log\nL7@P1: From the Editorial Supervisor\nL8@P1: Mariners Weather Log\nL9@P1: Mariners Weather Log\nL10@P1: U.S. Department of Commerce\nL11@P1: William M. Daley, Secretary\nL12@P1: National Oceanic and\nL13@P1: Atmospheric Administration\nL14@P1: Dr. D. James Baker, Administrator\nL15@P1: National Weather Service\nL16@P1: John J. Kelly, Jr.,\nL17@P1: Assistant Administrator for Weather Services\nL18@P1: National Environmental Satellite,\nL19@P1: Data, and Information Service\nL20@P1: Robert S. Winokur,\nL21@P1: Assistant Administrator\nL22@P1: United States Navy\nL23@P1: Naval Meteorology and Oceanography Command\nL24@P1: RADM Kenneth E. Barbor USN, Commander\nL25@P1: Editorial Supervisor\nL26@P1: Martin S. Baron\nL27@P1: Editor\nL28@P1: Mary Ann Burke\nL29@P1: The Secretary of Commerce has determined that the publication of this\nL30@P1: periodical is necessary in the transaction of the public business required by law\nL31@P1: of this department. Use of funds for printing this periodical has been approved\nL32@P1: by the director of the Office of Management and Budget through December\nL33@P1: 1999.\nL34@P1: The Mariners Weather Log (ISSN: 0025-3367) is published by the National\nL35@P1: Weather Service, Office of Meteorology, Integrated Hydrometeorological\nL36@P1: Services Core, Silver Spring, Maryland, (301) 713-1677, Ext. 134. Funding is\nL37@P1: provided by the National Weather Service, National Environmental Satellite,\nL38@P1: Data, and Information Service, and the United States Navy. Data is provided by\nL39@P1: the National Climatic Data Center.\nL40@P1: Articles, photographs, and letters should be sent to:\nL41@P1: Mr. Martin S. Baron, Editorial Supervisor\nL42@P1: Mariners Weather Log\nL43@P1: National Weather Service, NOAA\nL44@P1: 1325 East-West Highway, Room 14108\nL45@P1: Silver Spring, MD 20910\nL46@P1: Phone: (301) 713-1677 Ext. 134\nL47@P1: Fax: (301) 713-1598\nL48@P1: E-mail: martin.baron@noaa.gov\nL49@P1: Due to increased printing costs, the annual subscription\nL50@P1: price of the Mariners Weather Log is now $12.00 (domes\u0002tic) and $15.00 (foreign). Please see the inside back cover\nL51@P1: for the ordering form and more information.\nL52@P1: We thank those free subscribers who filled out and\nL53@P1: returned the questionnaires to us. However, some question\u0002naires have not yet been returned. If your vessel has\nL54@P1: changed crews, or has been in the yard for service, the\nL55@P1: questionnaire may be aboard without your knowledege (it’s\nL56@P1: a white card, folded size 5.5 x 8.5 inches). Please make\nL57@P1: every effort to complete and return these to us. You must\nL58@P1: do so to remain a free subscriber.\nL59@P1: This issue features an article on the endangered right\nL60@P1: whales in the North Atlantic. Effective July 1, 1999,\nL61@P1: vessels of 300 gross tons or greater are required to report\nL62@P1: data such as position, course, and speed when entering two\nL63@P1: right whale aggregation areas: one off Massachusettes and\nL64@P1: one off Georgia and Florida, as part of an International\nL65@P1: Maritime Organization approved effort to save this\nL66@P1: endangered species. Please see the article for details.\nL67@P1: For Voluntary Observing Ships, development of SEAS\nL68@P1: 2000 has begun. This is a new Windows-based program to\nL69@P1: facilitate reporting of meteorological observations. The\nL70@P1: projected release date is early 2000. I reviewed a pre\u0002production version and was very impressed. Three NOAA\nL71@P1: line offices are collaborating in this effort. It’s being lead\nL72@P1: by the Office of Atmospheric Research, Global Ocean\nL73@P1: Observing System (GOOS) Operations Center, with the\nL74@P1: office of NOAA Corps Operations writing the software, in\nL75@P1: cooperation with the National Weather Service. Prior to\nL76@P1: release of this new software, we recommend use of SEAS\nL77@P1: version 4.52, which is alsoY2K compliant, available from\nL78@P1: PMOs, SEAS Field Representavives, or the SEAS\nL79@P1: webpage at: http://seas.nos.noaa.gov/seas/.\nL80@P1: Martin S. Baronh\nL81@P1: Some Important Webpage Addresses\nL82@P1: NOAA http://www.noaa.gov\nL83@P1: National Weather Service http://www.nws.noaa.gov\nL84@P1: VOS Program http://www.vos.noaa.gov\nL85@P1: SEAS Program http://seas.nos.noaa.gov/seas/\nL86@P1: Mariners Weather Log http://www.nws.noaa.gov/om/\nL87@P1:  mwl/mwl.htm\nL88@P1: Marine Dissemination http://www.nws.noaa.gov/om/\nL89@P1:  marine/home.htm\nL90@P1-2: See these webpages for further links.April 1999 3\nL91@P2: Mariners Weather Log\nL92@P2: Table of Contents\nL93@P2: Hurricane Mitch—One of the Deadliest Atlantic Hurricanes in History ....................................... 4\nL94@P2: Civil War Naval Scenes of Xanthus Smith ..................................................................................... 7\nL95@P2: The Saffir/Simpson Hurricane Scale: An Interview with Dr. Robert Simpson ........................... 10\nL96@P2: AMVER Safety Network .............................................................................................................. 13\nL97@P2: Departments:\nL98@P2: Physical Oceanography ..................................................................................................................................... 17\nL99@P2: National Data Buoy Center................................................................................................................................ 23\nL100@P2: National Marine Fisheries Service ..................................................................................................................... 26\nL101@P2: Marine Weather Review\nL102@P2: North Atlantic, August–November 1998............................................................................................. 33\nL103@P2: North Pacific, August–November 1998 .............................................................................................. 38\nL104@P2: Tropical Atlantic and Tropical East Pacific, September–December 1998 .......................................... 44\nL105@P2: Climate Prediction Center, September–December 1998 ..................................................................... 57\nL106@P2: Fam Float ........................................................................................................................................................... 59\nL107@P2: Coastal Forecast Office News ............................................................................................................................ 60\nL108@P2: VOS Program .................................................................................................................................................... 61\nL109@P2: VOS Cooperative Ship Reports ......................................................................................................................... 81\nL110@P2: Buoy Climatological Data Summary ................................................................................................................. 95\nL111@P2: Meteorological Services\nL112@P2: Observations .......................................................................................................................................102\nL113@P2-3: Forecasts ............................................................................................................................................. 1054 Mariners Weather Log\nL114@P3: Hurricane Mitch\nL115@P3: Continued on Page 5\nL116@P3: Hurricane Mitch, the\nL117@P3: strongest October hurri\u0002cane ever recorded,\nL118@P3: formed in the southwest Carib\u0002bean Sea from a tropical wave\nL119@P3: about 440 miles south of\nL120@P3: Kingston, Jamaica late on October\nL121@P3: 21, 1998. The system initially\nL122@P3: moved slowly westward and\nL123@P3: intensified into a tropical storm on\nL124@P3: October 22, while located about\nL125@P3: 260 miles east-southeast of San\nL126@P3: Andres Island. Mitch then moved\nL127@P3: slowly northward, and then north\u0002northwestward on the 23rd and\nL128@P3: 24th while gradually gaining\nL129@P3: strength. Early on October 24,\nL130@P3: Mitch became a hurricane and was\nL131@P3: Hurricane Mitch\nL132@P3: One of the Deadliest Atlantic Hurricanes in History\nL133@P3: John L. Guiney and Richard J. Pasch\nL134@P3: Tropical Prediction Center\nL135@P3: National Hurricane Center\nL136@P3: Miami, Florida\nL137@P3: centered about 350 miles east\u0002southeast of Cabo Gracias a Dios,\nL138@P3: Nicaragua. Later that day, as it\nL139@P3: turned toward the west, Mitch\nL140@P3: began to intensify rapidly. In about\nL141@P3: 24 hours its central pressure\nL142@P3: dropped 52 mb, to 924 mb, by the\nL143@P3: afternoon of October 25. Further\nL144@P3: strengthening took place and the\nL145@P3: central pressure reached a mini\u0002mum of 905 mb on the afternoon\nL146@P3: of October 26, while the hurricane\nL147@P3: was centered about 35 nm south\u0002east of Swan Island (see cover\nL148@P3: photograph). This pressure is the\nL149@P3: fourth lowest pressure ever\nL150@P3: measured in an Atlantic hurricane\nL151@P3: (tied with Hurricane Camille in\nL152@P3: 1969). This is also the lowest\nL153@P3: pressure ever observed in an\nL154@P3: October hurricane in the Atlantic\nL155@P3: basin. At its peak on the 26th,\nL156@P3: Mitch’s maximum winds were\nL157@P3: estimated to be 155 knots, making\nL158@P3: it a category five hurricane on the\nL159@P3: Saffir/Simpson Hurricane Scale.\nL160@P3: After passing over Swan Island,\nL161@P3: Mitch began to gradually weaken\nL162@P3: on October 27 while moving\nL163@P3: slowly west. It then turned south\u0002westward and southward toward\nL164@P3: the Bay Islands off the coast of\nL165@P3: Honduras. The center passed very\nL166@P3: near the Island of Guanaja as a\nL167@P3-4: category four hurricane, wreakingApril 1999 5\nL168@P4: Hurricane Mitch\nL169@P4: Hurricane Mitch\nL170@P4: Continued from Page 4\nL171@P4: havoc there. Mitch slowly weak\u0002ened as its circulation interacted\nL172@P4: with the land mass of Honduras.\nL173@P4: From mid-day on the 27th to early\nL174@P4: on the 29th, the central pressure\nL175@P4: rose 59 mb. The center of the\nL176@P4: hurricane meandered near the\nL177@P4: north coast of Honduras from late\nL178@P4: on the 27th through the 28th,\nL179@P4: before making landfall during the\nL180@P4: morning of the 29th about 60 nm\nL181@P4: east of La Ceiba with 85-knot\nL182@P4: winds. Mitch moved southward\nL183@P4: over Honduras, weakening to a\nL184@P4: tropical storm early on the 30th.\nL185@P4: The cyclone moved slowly over\nL186@P4: Honduras and Guatemala on\nL187@P4: October 30-31, gradually weaken\u0002ing to a tropical depression. Mitch\nL188@P4: generated torrential rains over\nL189@P4: portions of Honduras and Nicara\u0002gua, where the associated floods\nL190@P4: and mud slides were devastating.\nL191@P4: The highest rainfall total reported\nL192@P4: by the Honduras Weather Service\nL193@P4: was 35.89 inches in Choluteca,\nL194@P4: located in the southernmost\nL195@P4: portion of the country. Even\nL196@P4: higher values may have gone\nL197@P4: unobserved. Some heavy rains\nL198@P4: also occurred in neighboring\nL199@P4: countries.\nL200@P4: Although Mitch’s surface circula\u0002tion center dissipated near the\nL201@P4: Guatemala/southeast Mexico\nL202@P4: border on November 1, the\nL203@P4: remnant circulation aloft contin\u0002ued to produce locally heavy\nL204@P4: rainfall over portions of central\nL205@P4: America and eastern Mexico for\nL206@P4: the next couple of days. On\nL207@P4: November 3, a low-level circula\u0002tion became evident in the eastern\nL208@P4: Bay of Campeche and the system\nL209@P4: regenerated into a tropical storm\nL210@P4: while located about 150 miles\nL211@P4: southwest of Merida, Mexico.\nL212@P4: Mitch moved northeastward and\nL213@P4: weakened to a depression early on\nL214@P4: the 4th as it moved inland over the\nL215@P4: northwest Yucatan peninsula. The\nL216@P4: center re-emerged over the south\u0002central Gulf of Mexico by mid\u0002morning on the 4th, and Mitch\nL217@P4: regained tropical storm strength.\nL218@P4: The storm began to accelerate\nL219@P4: northeastward as it became\nL220@P4: involved with a frontal zone\nL221@P4: moving through the eastern Gulf.\nL222@P4: Mitch made landfall on the\nL223@P4: morning of November 5 in south\u0002west Florida near Naples, with\nL224@P4: maximum sustained winds near 55\nL225@P4: knots. By mid-afternoon of the\nL226@P4: 5th, Mitch moved offshore of\nL227@P4: south Florida, and became extra\u0002tropical.\nL228@P4: Most ships heeded the marine\nL229@P4: forecasts and only 30 ships\nL230@P4: reported wind of 34 knots or\nL231@P4: greater during hurricane Mitch.\nL232@P4: Table 1 lists these reports along\nL233@P4: with the pressure and significant\nL234@P4: wave height. The highest wind\nL235@P4: was 54 knots reported by ship\nL236@P4: C6HH3 (16.2N, 87.6W) at 1500\nL237@P4: UTC on 31 October.\nL238@P4: It has been estimated that there\nL239@P4: was a 50 percent loss to Hondu\u0002ras’ agricultural crops. At least\nL240@P4: 70,000 houses were damaged and\nL241@P4: more than 92 bridges were dam\u0002aged or destroyed. There was\nL242@P4: severe damage to the infrastruc\u0002ture of Honduras and entire\nL243@P4: communities were isolated from\nL244@P4: outside assistance. To a lesser\nL245@P4: extent, damage was similar in\nL246@P4: Nicaragua, where a large mudslide\nL247@P4: inundated ten communities\nL248@P4: situated at the base of La Casitas\nL249@P4: Volcano. Guatemala and El\nL250@P4: Salvador also suffered from flash\nL251@P4: floods which destroyed thousands\nL252@P4: of homes, along with bridges and\nL253@P4: roads.\nL254@P4: The estimated death toll from\nL255@P4: Mitch (as of February 1,1999)\nL256@P4: stands at 9,086, with a comparable\nL257@P4: number of missing persons. The\nL258@P4: greatest losses of life occurred in\nL259@P4: Honduras and Nicaragua—5,677\nL260@P4: and 2,836, respectively. The death\nL261@P4: toll also includes 31 fatalities\nL262@P4: associated with the loss of the\nL263@P4: schooner FANTOME. The exact\nL264@P4: number of deaths caused by Mitch\nL265@P4: will probably never be known.\nL266@P4: However, this was one of the\nL267@P4: deadliest Atlantic tropical cy\u0002clones in history, ranking below\nL268@P4: only the 1780 “Great Hurricane”\nL269@P4: in the Lesser Antilles, but compa\u0002rable to the Galveston hurricane of\nL270@P4: 1900, and Hurricane Fifi of 1974,\nL271@P4: the latter also striking Honduras.\nL272@P4: Most of the U.S. damage from\nL273@P4: Mitch was caused by tornadoes in\nL274@P4: the Florida Keys which injured 65\nL275@P4: people, damaged or destroyed 645\nL276@P4: homes, and caused an estimated\nL277@P4-5: $40 million in damages.h6 Mariners Weather Log\nL278@P5: Hurricane Mitch\nL279@P5: Table 1. Hurricane Mitch Ship Reports ≥ 34 Knots\nL280@P5: Date/ Sust.\nL281@P5: Press Time Wind Max. Significant\nL282@P5: Location (mb) (UTC) (kts) Wave Height (FT)\nL283@P5: PFRO (14.4N 77.0W) 1010.2 22/1200 37 2.0\nL284@P5: ZCBN5 (11.8N 78.3W) 1006.0 23/2100 38 2.0\nL285@P5: ZCBN5 (12.5N 77.6W) 1005.2 24/0000 37 2.0\nL286@P5: ZCBN5 (13.4N 77.1W) 1005.3 24/0300 40 MM\nL287@P5: ZCBN5 (14.2N 76.7W) 1006.1 24/0600 39 MM\nL288@P5: PEXV (19.7N 81.3W) 1009.1 25/2100 43 2.0\nL289@P5: PDWT (20.2N 84.3W) 1008.0 27/0000 37 3.0\nL290@P5: KGDF (21.5N 76.5W) 1012.0 27/0000 35 3.0\nL291@P5: 3FKZ3 (22.1N 73.1W) 1016.0 27/0000 36 3.0\nL292@P5: PDWT (20.4N 83.9W) 1009.5 27/0300 39 7.0\nL293@P5: PDWT (20.6N 83.5W) 1009.5 27/0600 39 MM\nL294@P5: PDWT (20.7N 83.0W) 1009.0 27/0900 45 MM\nL295@P5: PJAG (9.6N 85.5W) 1011.0 27/1200 39 2.0\nL296@P5: PDWT (20.8N 82.5W) 1012.0 27/1200 37 4.0\nL297@P5: ELRU3 (21.1N 85.5W) 1010.0 27/1200 37 MM\nL298@P5: C6YC (21.3N 83.2W) 1010.0 27/1800 40 2.0\nL299@P5: C6YC (20.9N 82.6W) 1009.5 27/2100 45 3.0\nL300@P5: C6KU7 (18.6N 86.6W) 1005.1 28/1200 40 3.0\nL301@P5: PJAG (14.4N 77.3W) 1010.0 31/1200 35 2.0\nL302@P5: C6YE (17.7N 87.2W) 1008.0 31/1200 38 3.0\nL303@P5: C6HH3 (16.2N 87.6W) 1007.8 31/1500 54 MM\nL304@P5: WLDF (23.9N 86.9W) 1003.7 04/0600 40 4.0\nL305@P5: 3FKZ3 (20.3N 85.4W) 999.0 04/1200 48 MM\nL306@P5: WLDF (24.7N 84.9W) 1003.0 04/1200 39 2.0\nL307@P5: 3FKZ3 (20.0N 84.9W) 1000.0 04/1500 48 5.0\nL308@P5: 3FKZ3 (19.5N 82.8W) 1001.0 05/0000 36 4.0\nL309@P5: ELFT8 (23.2N 86.6W) 998.0 05/0000 38 2.0\nL310@P5: C6KY3 (22.7N 86.3W) 997.0 05/0300 40 3.0\nL311@P5: SHIP (25.1N 85.2W) 1000.5 05/0600 36 6.0\nL312@P5: KXDB (24.9N 80.3W) 996.1 05/1200 45 3.0\nL313@P5: C6KU7 (25.9N 77.5W) 1000.0 05/1500 35 3.0\nL314@P5: 3EZK9 (25.1N 75.6W) 1001.0 05/1800 37 10.0\nL315@P5: ELUA5 (26.0N 75.4W) 1000.0 05/1800 38 4.0\nL316@P5: Note: Observed wind speeds and wave heights were relatively low because vessels heeded warnings and fled\nL317@P5-6: the area where the higher values would have been observed.April 1999 7\nL318@P6: Independence Seaport Museum\nL319@P6: Editors Note: The exhibit of Civil\nL320@P6: War Naval Scenes was on display\nL321@P6: at the Independence Seaport\nL322@P6: Museum in Philadelphia, Pennsyl\u0002vania, through May 30, 1999.\nL323@P6: I\nL324@P6: t is often said that the Civil\nL325@P6: War was fought on land, but\nL326@P6: won at sea. While this theory\nL327@P6: is true to a large extent, the\nL328@P6: prominent role that both the Union\nL329@P6: and Confederate navies played\nL330@P6: during this period in history is not\nL331@P6: nearly as well known as many of\nL332@P6: the more famous Civil War land\nL333@P6: battles such as Vicksburg and\nL334@P6: Gettysburg. Indeed, some of the\nL335@P6: most important conflicts occurred\nL336@P6: not only along the United States’\nL337@P6: Civil War Naval Scenes of Xanthus Smith\nL338@P6: Liz Barszczewski and Ed Lynch\nL339@P6: Independence Seaport Museum\nL340@P6: Philadelphia, Pennsylvania\nL341@P6: coastline, rivers, and inlets, but\nL342@P6: also on the high seas.\nL343@P6: Xanthus Smith was born on\nL344@P6: February 26, 1839, on Locust\nL345@P6: Street in Philadelphia, to Russell\nL346@P6: Smith, a renowned 19th-century\nL347@P6: theater curtain and scenery\nL348@P6: painter, and his wife, Mary Smith,\nL349@P6: a well-known naturalist who\nL350@P6: worked in watercolors. At a young\nL351@P6: age, Smith expressed an interest in\nL352@P6: the sea through his sketches and\nL353@P6: watercolors. After touring Europe\nL354@P6: in the 1850s, he returned to study\nL355@P6: drawing at The Pennsylvania\nL356@P6: Academy of the Fine Arts in\nL357@P6: Philadelphia.\nL358@P6: With the outbreak of the Civil\nL359@P6: War, Smith enlisted in the Navy\nL360@P6: and was stationed at Port Royal,\nL361@P6: South Carolina, aboard the U.S.S.\nL362@P6: WABASH, Rear Admiral Samuel\nL363@P6: Francis DuPont’s flagship of the\nL364@P6: South Atlantic Blockading Squad\u0002ron. It was during this assignment\nL365@P6: that Smith was encouraged by\nL366@P6: Rear Admiral DuPont and other\nL367@P6: superior officers to sketch and\nL368@P6: paint in detail several vessels in\nL369@P6: the squadron.\nL370@P6: In the latter part of 1864, Smith\nL371@P6: was forced to resign his commis\u0002sion and return home due to his\nL372@P6-7: Continued on Page 98 Mariners Weather Log\nL373@P7-8: Independence Seaport MuseumFarragut passing the forts below New Orleans. Xanthus Smith, 1872, oil on canvas. Courtesy Philadelphia Independence Seaport Museum (loan from Atwater Kent Museum).April 1999 9\nL374@P8: Independence Seaport Museum\nL375@P8: Xanthus Smith\nL376@P8: Continued from Page 7\nL377@P8: father’s poor health. However, he\nL378@P8: continued to create ship portraits\nL379@P8: similar to the ones he painted\nL380@P8: while stationed at Port Royal.\nL381@P8: These paintings led to the devel\u0002opment of his studied and accurate\nL382@P8: approach to well-known Civil War\nL383@P8: naval battles including the clash\nL384@P8: between MONITOR and\nL385@P8: MERRIMACK at Hampton\nL386@P8: Roads, South Carolina, and the\nL387@P8: battles of New Orleans and\nL388@P8: Mobile Bay.\nL389@P8: While Smith’s work focused on\nL390@P8: and represented individual battles,\nL391@P8: it also documented and conveyed\nL392@P8: important technological develop\u0002ments of the War. One of the most\nL393@P8: important developments reflected\nL394@P8: in his work was the emergence of\nL395@P8: an ironclad navy. It was the\nL396@P8: Confederates who first began\nL397@P8: ironclad construction with the\nL398@P8: conversion of U.S.S. MERRI\u0002MACK into the ironclad steamer\nL399@P8: C.S.S. VIRGINIA. Eventually,\nL400@P8: Union forces saw the need for an\nL401@P8: ironclad navy and began to work\nL402@P8: on the design and implementation\nL403@P8: of such vessels. Three very\nL404@P8: different designs were submitted\nL405@P8: to the Union Navy and approved\nL406@P8: for construction. Two of the three\nL407@P8: came from designer John Ericsson\nL408@P8: and shipbuilder Merrick and\nL409@P8: Sons’, both of Philadelphia.\nL410@P8: Ericsson’s design for the MONI\u0002TOR and Merrick and Sons’\nL411@P8: design for the hull which would\nL412@P8: later to be known as NEW\nL413@P8: IRONSIDES, were quickly put\nL414@P8: into production. This commitment\nL415@P8: to new technology had an invalu\u0002able impact on the future of the\nL416@P8: U.S. Navy and resulted in the\nL417@P8: construction of 40 Monitors by the\nL418@P8: end of the war. Eventually,\nL419@P8: Merrick and Sons’ NEW\nL420@P8: IRONSIDES emerged as the\nL421@P8: prototype of the modern day navy\nL422@P8: vessel.\nL423@P8: Much of Smith’s most famous and\nL424@P8: dramatic work recorded the\nL425@P8: frequent skirmishes with blockade\nL426@P8: runners along U.S. waterways. As\nL427@P8: a means of applying economic\nL428@P8: pressure on the South and attempt\u0002ing to stop their trading with\nL429@P8: Europe, President Lincoln an\u0002nounced the implementation of a\nL430@P8: Union blockade in April 1861,\nL431@P8: which ran from Alexandria,\nL432@P8: Virginia, to the Rio Grande River.\nL433@P8: Confederate “runners” were\nL434@P8: employed to break through the\nL435@P8: Union blockade and smuggle\nL436@P8: goods in and out of the South. The\nL437@P8: tantalizing lure of immense profits\nL438@P8: attracted men from all over the\nL439@P8: globe to the dangerous job of\nL440@P8: blockade running. As the Union\nL441@P8: blockade became more efficient,\nL442@P8: the profits from blockade running\nL443@P8: soared, as did the increased risks.\nL444@P8: Smith’s work also detailed the\nL445@P8: international aspects of the Civil\nL446@P8: War. One of the most famous\nL447@P8: battles in international waters he\nL448@P8: depicted was that of U.S.S.\nL449@P8: KEARSARGE and C.S.S. ALA\u0002BAMA on June 19, 1864. ALA\u0002BAMA had been traveling the\nL450@P8: globe attacking northern merchant\nL451@P8: ships and KEARSARGE had been\nL452@P8: searching for her along the\nL453@P8: northern European coast to the\nL454@P8: Canaries, Madeira, and into the\nL455@P8: Western Islands since March\nL456@P8: 1863. The two finally met in the\nL457@P8: waters off Cherbourg, France.\nL458@P8: ALABAMA opened fire first.\nL459@P8: KEARSARGE held steady and\nL460@P8: closed the distance between the\nL461@P8: two ships to less than 1,000 yards\nL462@P8: before opening fire. Within the\nL463@P8: hour, ALABAMA was forced to\nL464@P8: strike her colors and admit defeat.\nL465@P8: She appealed for assistance from\nL466@P8: KEARSARGE, which rescued the\nL467@P8: majority of her crew.\nL468@P8: Throughout his life, Smith contin\u0002ued to paint numerous Civil War\nL469@P8: naval battles, some of which were\nL470@P8: unfamiliar to him. For these\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nJetStream Max: Tsunamis vs. Wind Waves | National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration (https://www.noaa.gov/jetstream/tsunamis/tsunami-propagation/jetstream-max-tsunamis-vs-wind-waves)\nciteturn22view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.noaa.gov/jetstream/tsunamis/tsunami-propagation/jetstream-max-tsunamis-vs-wind-waves\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 126\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFLIP Definition & Meaning - Merriam-Webster (https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/flip)\nciteturn22view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/flip\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 402\n"
